WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:03.460
What will you never get a second chance for?

00:00:03.800 --> 00:00:06.660
To create the first impression!

00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:09.760
We humans are generally too judgmental.

00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:10.980
Aren’t we?

00:00:11.220 --> 00:00:13.900
Why do we come to a conclusion based on our initial

00:00:13.900 --> 00:00:16.120
feeling or impression?

00:00:16.460 --> 00:00:20.100
Who or what influences us in our decision making?

00:00:20.360 --> 00:00:25.600
Let’s find that out!

00:00:25.900 --> 00:00:29.000
There is a psychology behind our initial impression.

00:00:29.360 --> 00:00:31.640
It’s called the Halo effect .

00:00:31.940 --> 00:00:34.660
It is explained as the behavior where we evaluate

00:00:34.660 --> 00:00:37.180
other qualities of a person or a thing

00:00:37.360 --> 00:00:39.860
based on their single quality.

00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:41.020
How?

00:00:41.340 --> 00:00:43.400
Let’s understand this.

00:00:43.720 --> 00:00:45.700
Imagine you’re streaming music online

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:48.340
and you loved the voice of a particular singer.

00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:52.780
Now mentally, you start creating an image of that person.

00:00:53.080 --> 00:00:55.160
She might be beautiful, may be she has

00:00:55.160 --> 00:00:58.440
high mental and emotional quotients, and so on.

00:00:58.880 --> 00:01:02.580
Here, the Halo effect has come into action unknowingly.

00:01:03.040 --> 00:01:05.400
No matter what her other qualities are,

00:01:05.620 --> 00:01:10.740
your perception about her is based entirely on her voice.

00:01:11.120 --> 00:01:14.580
We judge a movie by its trailer, don’t we?

00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:16.560
That’s the Halo effect.

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:20.020
A two minute trailer has the power to influence us

00:01:20.020 --> 00:01:21.940
into watching a movie.

00:01:22.260 --> 00:01:25.120
We even judge a video by its thumbnail.

00:01:25.120 --> 00:01:27.360
Funny, isn’t it?

00:01:27.720 --> 00:01:32.080
Famous personalities endorsing products works right?

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:35.140
That's because of the Halo effect.

00:01:35.140 --> 00:01:36.400
How?

00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:38.240
You admire your role model

00:01:38.320 --> 00:01:41.240
and have a positive impression about them.

00:01:41.500 --> 00:01:43.880
And this in turn makes us have similar

00:01:43.880 --> 00:01:47.240
positive impressions about the endorsed products.

00:01:47.660 --> 00:01:50.640
And we end up buying them!

00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:55.300
Now the question is, how does the Halo effect occur ?

00:01:55.620 --> 00:01:59.280
Well, our brain usually takes shortcuts.

00:01:59.500 --> 00:02:01.100
What does this mean?

00:02:01.380 --> 00:02:03.660
Analyzing a lot of complex information

00:02:03.660 --> 00:02:06.280
is a very difficult task for our brains.

00:02:06.620 --> 00:02:10.400
So, instead of looking into every quality of a person or a thing,

00:02:10.760 --> 00:02:13.940
our brain picks up only the most prominent quality

00:02:14.140 --> 00:02:19.160
and predicts the nature of other qualities based on one single quality.

00:02:19.520 --> 00:02:24.280
Does we end up taking biased decisions.

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:29.120
We know that we all have experienced the Halo effect at some point.

00:02:29.380 --> 00:02:32.960
We sometimes even know that we are judging a book by its cover.

00:02:33.320 --> 00:02:35.060
But we still do it.

00:02:35.580 --> 00:02:38.280
Do you think we can overcome the Halo effect?

00:02:38.900 --> 00:02:43.000
To be precise, can we control our first impression behaviour?

00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:45.280
Is it possible?

00:02:45.720 --> 00:02:48.760
If it occurs so quickly and unknowingly,

00:02:49.180 --> 00:02:52.280
can we prevent our brains from taking quick judgements?

00:02:52.880 --> 00:02:54.720
What do you think?

